id: pm-3s4jwl
title: Fields an extension registers through registerItemFields are not checked against built-in options of the commands they reach
description: "The option-collision refusal covers schema add-field, profile apply, profile lint and validate. A package that calls registerItemFields with a name equal to a built-in option is not refused at activation, and package doctor reports only the MCP input collision for it. Whether such a field can shadow a CLI option depends on how extension fields are exposed (the repeatable field assignment flag rather than a per-field option), which has not been measured."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 3
tags[3]: "area:sdk",extensions,schema
created_at: "2026-11-22T20:02:44.365Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-23T11:57:44.676Z"
closed_at: "2026-11-23T09:40:06.338Z"
completed_at: "2026-11-23T09:40:06.338Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 90
acceptance_criteria: "Measure whether an extension field named like a built-in option changes that option's behaviour on any command; If it does, activation or package doctor reports the collision using the shared collision helper; If it does not, the item records the evidence and the SDK doc states that extension fields are exempt"
parent: pm-ugqx
risk: low
confidence: medium
resolution: "Measured on the built CLI: extension fields named full, limit and json leave pm list --full, pm list --limit and pm search --full working because they are set through --field name=value; docs/EXTENSIONS.md states the exemption and the --output-include mode overlap. mutateWorkspaceSettings now runs assertFieldDefinitionOptionsAvailable on new or changed inline field definitions before writing (docs/SDK_AUDITED_MUTATIONS.md). Reserved-key bricking filed as pm-dhlij3."
expected_result: "Either a measured collision is reported at activation or by package doctor, or recorded evidence shows extension fields cannot shadow a built-in option"
actual_result: "Evidence recorded in a comment; settings-field-admission.spec.ts covers refused collisions and split flags for dry run and apply, an accepted free field, and a legacy definition kept through an unrelated write but refused once changed"
dependencies[2]{id,kind,created_at,author,source_kind,author_source}:
  pm-pta8um,discovered_from,"2026-11-22T20:02:44.365Z","harness:claude-code","cli:create:dep",detected
  pm-x9r2h3,related,"2026-11-23T11:57:43.679Z","harness:claude-code","cli:update:dep",detected
comments[4]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-22T21:23:54.120Z","harness:claude-code","Scope addition from the review of PR 1768 (2026-11-22): the extension settings mutation (mutateWorkspaceSettings) can append a field definition to the inline schema fields in settings, and that path only shape-validates the proposal, so a colliding definition can be stored there too. It belongs with this item: decide whether settings writers share the admission check for new or changed definitions while leaving unrelated writes able to preserve legacy definitions. validate already reports such a stored field.\n"
  "2026-11-23T08:47:06.302Z","harness:claude-code","Measured 2026-11-23 on the 2026.11.23 build. A project extension registering item fields full, limit and json leaves pm list --full (projection mode full), pm list --limit 2 (count 2, applied_limit 2) and pm search --full working as before; extension fields are set through the repeatable --field name=value assignment, not a per-field option, so no CLI option is shadowed and the add-field collision check does not apply. pm list --fields full and pm get <id> --fields full read the value. The one overlap is the projection-mode vocabulary of --output-include: pm get <id> --output-include full,limit reads full as the full projection mode and refuses projection_options_mutually_exclusive, so such a field is selected with --fields. docs/EXTENSIONS.md now states both.\n\nScope addition delivered: mutateWorkspaceSettings (src/sdk/extension-command-context.ts) runs assertFieldDefinitionOptionsAvailable on every inline schema field definition the callback adds or changes, dry run included, before anything is written; a definition left exactly as stored is not judged, so an unrelated write keeps a legacy definition (validate keeps reporting it). Spec: tests/unit/sdk/transactions/settings-field-admission.spec.ts.\n\nSide finding filed as pm-dhlij3: an extension field named priority (reserved) activates and then makes every create and update exit 2 with command_failed, and package doctor names only the MCP collision.\n"
  "2026-11-23T10:55:43.610Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 2 on PR #1772 (Codex and CodeRabbit, 2026-11-23). (1) A JavaScript settings proposal that omits schema or schema.fields, which validateSettings accepts, made the new admission throw on .filter; it is now read as no proposed fields. (2) A flag or alias that normalizes to nothing (cli_flag '--', alias '  ') made the registry drop the definition or alias silently, so the admission saw nothing; assertFieldDefinitionOptionsAvailable now refuses such raw spellings with schema_field_flag_invalid before resolving (normalizeCliToken exported from runtime-schema for it). settings-field-admission.spec.ts covers both.\n"
  "2026-11-23T11:57:30.699Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 3 on PR #1772 (Codex pass 3, 2026-11-23). A callback that JSON round-trips the settings tree drops undefined properties from the normalized field definitions, so an unchanged legacy inline field compared as changed and an unrelated write (author_default) was refused with schema_field_option_collision. Definitions now compare in their persisted JSON representation. Negative control: comparing the raw objects fails the new round-trip case in settings-field-admission.spec.ts. Validate reporting of definitions the registry drops (empty cli_flag, blank alias) is filed as pm-x9r2h3.\n"
files[4]:
